<h2>The jurisdiction invoked</h2>
<p>The petition travelled under Section 528 BNSS, the successor to Section 482 CrPC, which preserves the High Court&rsquo;s inherent power to make such orders as are necessary to prevent abuse of process and to secure the ends of justice. A trial that cannot finish is a standing failure of both. The Court anchored the direction in the Article 21 jurisprudence on speedy trial — the line of Supreme Court authority recognising that unreasonable delay in criminal proceedings infringes personal liberty, which the order cited in terms — and noted the petitioner&rsquo;s age: sixty-three, a senior citizen who was in her forties when the FIR was registered.</p>

<h2>Why delay directions matter, and to whom</h2>
<p>It is tempting to read speedy-trial rights as an accused-friendly doctrine, but a nineteen-year cheating trial serves no one. The complainant waits a generation for an adjudication of the alleged fraud. Witnesses scatter, memories thin, and documents age into illegibility, so whichever side eventually wins obtains a verdict built on degraded evidence. The accused lives under open charges through what may be the greater part of a working life — a burden the Supreme Court has repeatedly recognised as punishment in fact, whatever the eventual verdict. Directions of the present kind do not decide the case for either side; they restore the one condition on which any just outcome depends, which is that there be an outcome.</p>

<h2>Using the remedy well</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Step</th><th>What to show</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Document the delay</td><td>A dated chart of the proceedings: framing of charge, each witness&rsquo;s examination span, adjournments and their causes</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Show the arithmetic</td><td>Witnesses cited versus examined, and the projected end-date at the current pace</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Identify personal factors</td><td>Age, health, and the length of time the petitioner has lived under charge</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Seek a workable direction</td><td>Expedition with an outer timeframe — courts prefer &ldquo;preferably within one year&rdquo; to rigid guillotines that collide with trial realities</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Two cautions round out the picture. First, a party whose own adjournments built the delay will find the record turned against them — these petitions are won by the side with clean hands on the cause-list history. Second, the direction binds the schedule, not the result: the trial court retains full independence on the merits, and an expedition order is no comment on guilt or innocence.</p>
<div class="note">This note summarises an order of the Delhi High Court for general information and is not advice on any particular matter.</div>
